A||||8||LZeitgeist||Formula Clutch Job - Version 4.0 (dammit...)||||1||
Z||000000||LZeitgeist||09-29-2001||05:57 PM||||O.K., time for a poll...<P>In the opinions of this incredibly experienced and knowledgeable assembly here on these lists, what is the best clutch that can be bought for a 1988 Formula V-6/5-speed Getrag setup? The engine and transmission are all completely stock - I am planning no performance modifications to either - I run stock sized tires on stock wheels.<P>I certainly don't baby the car, but I don't thrash it, either. I would rather err towards the thrashing side, though, because  I want a clutch that will provide long-lasting and excellent performance, so I don't mind getting more clutch than I need. At this point, for this particular poll, money is no object since I would like the benefit of the widest range of experience possible. (I will be installing this myself, so I don't need shop recommendations or anything like that... just clutch info.)<P>If it was your Formula, what would you get? *That's* what I need to know. <P>Thanks to all in advance...<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rick 88||09-29-2001||08:39 PM||||I used a Centerforce clutch in my white 88 GT. The pedal pressure was less than the factory clutch and it engaged very nicely. I have heard others say it is not good. This has not been my experience with the Centerforce. I had considered a GM clutch but a number of people told me that their orignal clutches failed in the 20 to 30k range. <P>Rick<BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||09-29-2001||09:06 PM||||I've been happy with the stock clutch replacements I've gotten at the local auto parts stores over the years.  The factory clutch in my GT lasted nearly 140,000 miles before the disc lining disintegrated, and the last clutch I put in my 84 went about 80,000 miles before it had to be changed.  I don't baby the cars at all and enjoy rather spirited driving.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||09-29-2001||09:48 PM||||Thanks for the initial responses... I'll give a little more info...<P>About a year ago, the (original?) clutch crapped out on my Formula - the sleeve that the throwout bearing rides on snapped off at its base, and the clutch release fingers became ruined because of uneven pressure from the throwout bearing. At the time, I needed an immediate fix, so we used the seemingly brand-new clutch from the pile of parts we had from a junked '87 GT we had. That clutch lasted a week before every last speck of lining material one one side of the clutch disc turned into a pile of grated cheese in the bottom of the bellhousing. So, the following weekend, we did it again, this time going with what Advance Auto had in stock - a name brand I'd never heard of and don't remember, at a price of $129 for the clutch/PP/TO bearing set. <P>The third clutch swap I was involved in was with Formula88's clutch when he got his car. I can't remember what he put in his - I need to ask him if he doesn't post here soon.  <P>So, this makes the fourth (and hopefully final) one for me, so this time I want to do it right. I don't want to have to touch the transmission for at least 100,000 miles after this, 'cuz it's just too big a PITA. <P>The name I hear most often has been Centerforce, but since I haven't been in the market for another clutch, I haven't paid attention to whether people have been cussing Centerforce clutches or praising them. But, as I said, I'm open to all experiences and opinions, as my instincts were evidently in error in choosing what was readily available. (Ironically enough, my first car, an '88 4-cyl coupe, currently has 256,000 miles on it, and the only time the any clutch work had been done on it was back at 70,000 miles... wish I knew what kinda clutch went in *that* one 9 years ago...)<P><BR>Keep 'em coming, and thanks again...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||09-30-2001||07:05 AM||||Stock replacement is Borg-Warner. I've had several problems with some different clutches in the past and this is all I will use in ANYTHING from now on. I hate doing things twice in rapid succession!||||reg||1
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||09-30-2001||09:46 AM||||Now that you mention it, Borg-Warner is the other name I've heard quite often... can't say that I've heard anything bad about them...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||09-30-2001||11:58 PM||||Well, Borg-Warner or Borg & Beck are the OEM brands.  (Same company, different names/ownerships).  I'm using the Ram clutch from the Fiero Store (the HD, not the Racing Clutch) and so far (about 15K miles) it's been fine.  It seems to grip pretty good and I don't baby my car either (I've got a broken transmission mount as proof!).  I might go for something a bit more heavy duty next time around, but the Ram seems to be a good "stock" replacement.  I wouldn't want to put it to a torture test with high horsepower mods or N20.||||reg||4
Z||000007||Archie||10-01-2001||07:22 AM||||I have a late model used Clutch for sale, it only has 2 miles on it. It is said to be Computer tested to hold 500 HP V-8 engines, it should last forever on a Stock V-6 Fiero.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FShop-clutch_1.jpg"> <P>Archie<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||10-01-2001||08:54 AM||||ROTFLMFAO!!!!<P>Yeah, that's the ticket!  Normally, I'd be concerned about such a high performance clutch grabbing too hard and chattering, but that's not a problem with this one, huh, Archie?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
